# .gitattributes — LF is a HARD REQUIREMENT here, not a style preference.
# Do not delete this file. Do not narrow the `*` rule below to `*.sh`.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
# Claude Code installs this plugin by `git clone` into its marketplace cache, and
# that clone inherits the machine's `core.autocrlf` (the cache repo sets nothing
# of its own). The DEFAULT Git for Windows installer option is
# `core.autocrlf=true`. Without the rules below, every shipped script is checked
# out with CRLF on such a machine, and then:
#
#   plugins/mega-sdd/hooks/pre-tool-use   set -uo pipefail
#
# parses as `set -u -o $'pipefail\r'` -> `set: pipefail: invalid option name`.
# The hook exits RC=2 and evaluates NOTHING (v7.5.0: hooks.json dispatches each
# body DIRECTLY — there is no run-hook.sh dispatcher anymore, so every entry
# point is its own single point of failure). Both branches are severe:
#   * blocking hooks (PreToolUse, SessionStart, UserPromptExpansion,
#     UserPromptSubmit) fail the session outright;
#   * async hooks (PostToolUse, Stop) fail INVISIBLY — the dirty journal,
#     postflight scan and batch-suite gate all evaporate while the model still
#     believes it is gated.
# Independently, `plugins/mega-sdd/scripts/_lib/resolve-project-root.sh` takes a
# hard syntax error under CRLF, leaving `resolve_project_root` undefined for
# every script that sources it.
# Source: research/2026-07-29-windows-portability-audit.md, finding
# `crlf-no-gitattributes` (severity: hang, skills: ALL).
#
# WHY THE CATCH-ALL MUST STAY `*`
# The six hook entry points have NO file extension —
#   hooks/pre-tool-use, post-tool-use, session-start, stop,
#   user-prompt-submit, user-prompt-expansion
# — so a `*.sh` rule misses every single one of them, i.e. it would miss exactly
# the files whose corruption kills the plugin.
#
# LIMIT
# Adding this file does NOT renormalize an existing clone. A machine that already
# checked out CRLF must re-clone the plugin cache.
#
# ENFORCEMENT (gates > rules > hooks)
# tests/platform/test-line-endings.sh asks Git itself, via `git check-attr text
# eol`, whether eol=lf actually reaches all six extensionless entry points, and
# fails if any tracked file under plugins/mega-sdd/ carries a CR byte.

* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Binary / byte-exact exceptions ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Derived from `git ls-files`, not guessed: the only tracked files Git's own
# content heuristic classifies as binary today are three PDFs
# (docs/prd-reference/*.pdf, examples/timeoff/PRD-Examples.pdf). `text=auto`
# would already spare them via the NUL-byte heuristic; pinning them explicitly
# means no future heuristic miss can rewrite their bytes.
# If you ever track a new binary type (png/jpg/ico/woff/xlsx/zip/…), add it here.
*.pdf binary

# Byte-exact fixture: deliberately invalid UTF-8 (0xC3 0x28) with NO NUL byte, so
# Git's heuristic classifies it as TEXT and `text=auto` would take it. `-text`
# (rather than `binary`) pins its bytes while keeping it diffable — the fail-open
# regression it guards is a function of those exact bytes.
tests/fixtures/code-delivery/regressions/adv01-utf8-failopen/.mega-sdd/vaults/demo/04-flows.md -text
